	• Awk
	- Awk is a utility/language designed for data extraction. Most of the time it is used to extract fields from a file or from an output
	- $ awk --version
	- $ awk '{print $1}' filename # list 1st field from a file
	- $ ls -l | awk '{print $1,$3}' # list 1 and 3 fields of ls -l output
	- $ ls -l | awk '{print $NF}' # Last field of the output
	- $ awk '/word/ {print}' filename # Search for a specific word
	- $ awk -F: '{print $1)' filename # output 1st field of delimiter of the file
	- $ echo "Hello Tom" | awk '{$2="Adam"; print $0}' # replace words field words
	- $ awk 'length($0) > 15' file # Get lines that have more than 15 byte size
	- $ ls -l | awk '{if($9 == "searchword"} print $0;}' # Get the field matching searchword in home dir
	- $ ls -l | awk '{print NF}' # Number of fields

	Ø Pipes
	- A Pipe is used by the shell to connect the output of one command directly to the input of another command.
	The symbol for a pipe is the vertical bar (|). 
	The command syntax is: command1 [arguments] | command2 [arguments]
	$ cd /etc
	$ ls - ltr
	$ ls -ltr | more # more-one page at a time -> spacebar and q
	$ ll # ls -l
	$ ls -l | tail -1

	• Truncate File size (truncate)
	- The Linuz truncate command is often used to shrink or extend the size of a file to the specified size. (unlike compressing like gzip it will chop the data, the data might lost)
		○ $ truncate -s 10 filename

	• Combining and Splitting files
	- Multiple files can be combined into one and 
	- One file can be split into multiple files
		○ $ cat file1 file2 file3 > file4
		○ $ split file4
		○ $ split -l 300 file.txt childfile
	- Split file.txt into 300 lines per file and output to childfileaa, childfileab and childfileac

	Ø Executing multiple commands with ;
	- $ ls ; pwd; whoami
	- Works independently, even one fails the next one continues to run
	- $ echo "Hello" ; echo "World"
	- $ mkdir test_dir; cd test_dir; touch test_filte.txt
	- $ ls
	- $ ls ; invalid_command; echo "completed"
	
	

	• Grep/egrep
	- The grep command which stands for " global regular expression print" processes text line by line and prints any lines which match a specified pattern
	- $ grep --version
	- $ grep -- help
	- $ man grep
	- $ grep keyword filename # Search for a keyword from a file
	- $ grep -c keyword filename # search for a keyword and count it
	- $ grep -i keyword file # search for a keyword ignore case-sensitive
	- $ grep -n keyword filename # Display the matched lines and their line numbers
	- $ grep -v keyword filename # Display everything except keyword
	- $ grep keyword filename | awk '{print $1}' 
	- $ ls -l | grep Desktop


	- $ egrep -i "keyword1|keyword2" filename

